\description{
Check whether cohort labels meet conditions.
The \code{diagnose} functions report on each condition, and the
\code{assert} functions throw an error if a condition is not met.
\itemize{
\item \code{cohort_diagnose_values()} and \code{cohort_assert_values()} check the
elements of \code{labels}.
\item \code{cohort_diagnose_levels()} and \code{cohort_assert_levels()} check
the levels of \code{labels}, including any unused levels.
Both functions require \code{labels} to be a factor.
}

Conditions include:
\itemize{
\item Not overlapping
\item Not having gaps
\item Not having totals or missing values
\item Having, or not having, open cohorts
}
}
\section{Reporting trees}{


In a reporting tree, records are tabulated at different levels of
detail. A census table, for instance, might have detailed
cohorts, some broader cohorts for records with less detailed
information, and a category for records with no information.
The cohorts nest: any two either do not overlap, or one
contains the other. The most detailed cohorts (the leaves of
the tree) cover the full span, so that every broader cohort is
divided exactly into narrower ones. Leaves do not need to be
the same distance from the root: some broader cohorts can be
divided further than others.

Some tables use a label such as \code{"Total"} to mean that the
cohort is not stated. Recode such labels to \code{"Missing"},
for instance using \code{x[cohort_is_total(x)] <- "Missing"},
so that they are not treated as totals (for instance
by \code{no_total}).

To check trees separately for each group, use \code{\link[dplyr:group_by]{dplyr::group_by()}}.
}

\section{Rules for interpreting inputs}{


\strong{Single-year}

Labels for single-year cohorts sometimes refer to
the lower limits of cohorts, and sometimes
refer to upper limits. For instance, the label \code{"2020"}
is sometimes used to refer to the cohort that
starts at midnight on 1 January 2020
and ends at midnight on 1 January 2021,
and is sometimes used to refer to the
cohort that starts at midnight on 1 July 2019
and ends at midnight on 1 July 2020.

The \code{interpret_single} argument can be used to distinguish
between the two conventions:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{interpret_single} \tab \emph{Rule} \tab \emph{Example} \cr
   \code{"lower"} \tab \verb{"a" -> [a,a+1)} \tab \verb{"2020" -> [2020,2021)} \cr
   \code{"upper"} \tab \verb{"a" -> [a-1,a)} \tab \verb{"2020" -> [2019,2020)} \cr
}


\strong{Multi-year}

In labels for multi-year cohorts, the second number is
sometimes the upper limit of the cohort, and sometimes
the upper limit minus 1. For instance, the cohort
that starts at midnight on 1 January 2020 and ends
at midnight on 1 January 2025 is sometimes labelled
\code{"2020-2025"} and is sometimes labelled \code{"2020-2024"}.

The \code{interpret_multi} argument can be used to distinguish
between the two conventions:\tabular{lll}{
   \code{interpret_multi} \tab \emph{Rule} \tab \emph{Example} \cr
   \code{"at_upper"} \tab \verb{"a-b" -> [a,b)} \tab \verb{"2020-2025" -> [2020,2025)} \cr
   \code{"below_upper"} \tab \verb{"a-b" -> [a,b+1)} \tab \verb{"2020-2024" -> [2020,2025)} \cr
}


If \code{interpret_multi} is \code{NULL} (the default), then multi-year
labels are interpreted using the \code{"at_upper"} convention.
If, however, the \code{"at_upper"} convention
leads to intervals with
widths 4, 9, 14, \dots, then \pkg{agetime} emits a message.
Supplying an explicit value for \code{interpret_multi}
silences this message.

\strong{Open}

A cohort that is open on the left has no lower limit,
and a cohort that is open on the right has no upper limit.
Labels for open cohorts are not affected by \code{interpret_single}
or \code{interpret_multi}.

Some examples of open cohorts:\tabular{ll}{
   \emph{Label} \tab \emph{Interval} \cr
   \code{"<2000"}, \code{"before 2000"}, \code{"under 2000"} \tab \verb{(-Inf,2000)} \cr
   \code{"2000 or earlier"}, \code{"up to 2000"} \tab \verb{(-Inf,2001)} \cr
   \code{"2000+"}, \code{"2000 or more"}, \code{"2000 or later"} \tab \verb{[2000,Inf)} \cr
}


Labels starting with \code{"-"}, such as \code{"-2000"}, are not accepted.
}

